  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1200 PM MDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows broad upper ridging across the south \ncentral CONUS. Upper flow over the CWA is nearly zonal, but active \nWSW flow over the western CONUS is quickly approaching, along with \nmonsoonal moisture. A strong shortwave is over NW UT/eastern \nID/western WY pushing eastward toward the CWA. Forcing ahead of the \nwave is bringing showers and thunderstorms to much of WY, including \nNE WY into the Black Hills and far SW SD. Deep SE low level flow is \nkeeping moisture-rich air across the CWA even into NE WY, with \ndewpoints in the upper 50s to upper 60s areawide. MLCAPE of 1000-\n1500 J/kg is expected as the cap quickly erodes this afternoon. 0-\n6km shear will increase this afternoon to 40-60kt, supporting strong \nto severe storms, including isolated supercells. Large hail and \ndamaging winds will be the main threats. Heavy rainfall will also be \nsupported by PWATs of 150-175% of normal. CAMs show the current \nprecip continuing northeastward, then storms developing afterward by \nmid afternoon across NE WY and the Black Hills area...then pushing \neastward across the western SD plains. Generally quiet weather is \nexpected overnight tonight but could still see some linger showers \nthrough early Thursday morning. Upper flow becomes more \nsouthwesterly on Thursday as another strong wave crosses the region. \nThis will likely bring another round of high-coverage showers and \nthunderstorms. Instability will be slightly weaker tomorrow and \nshear will be much weaker, so organized severe weather is not \nexpected. However, with PWATS nearing 200% of normal, hoping to see \nmore much-needed precipitation though models are differing on QPF \namounts and placement. Moist monsoonal flow will keep daily chances \nfor storms across the region through the end of this and the \nweekend, along with near to slightly below average temps. Can't rule \nout strong/severe storms over the weekend.",
